1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,780
Peat is half-rotted plants piled up
in water over thousands of years.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:07,780
Kept wet, it behaves like a soaked
sponge that is hard to light.

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:09,780
Then come the ditches.

4
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:14,300
Canals are dug to pull the water out
so crops like oil palm or paper trees

5
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:14,900
can grow there.

6
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:17,733
The sponge dries from the top down.

7
00:00:18,633 --> 00:00:21,553
What once resisted fire turns
into a deep store of fuel.

8
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:27,372
A twenty twenty-five study of Sumatra
and Borneo compared peatland with and

9
00:00:27,372 --> 00:00:28,360
without canals.

10
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:32,744
Drained peat showed up to thirteen
times more fire hotspots per square

11
00:00:32,744 --> 00:00:33,420
kilometre.
